1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,619
This is ECE 320, video 13. The topic is interrupts and exceptions in the five stage pipeline.

2
00:00:09,069 --> 00:00:15,703
After this video, you can do four things. One: tell an exception, an interrupt and a trap apart.

3
00:00:16,153 --> 00:00:19,737
Two: explain precise exceptions and the commit point.

4
00:00:20,187 --> 00:00:25,201
Three: show which instructions finish, which are flushed, and which P C is saved.

5
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:33,267
Four: solve a pipeline design question on interrupts. It is question 3 of the spring 2020 quiz.

6
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:38,325
Start with a program. It runs i 1, then i 2, then i 3.

7
00:00:38,775 --> 00:00:46,625
An interrupt comes after i 1. Control goes to a handler. The handler is a routine outside the program.

8
00:00:47,075 --> 00:00:52,899
The hardware saves the P C of i 2. i 2 is the next instruction to run.

9
00:00:53,349 --> 00:00:58,149
The handler runs i h 1, i h 2, and so on, to its end.

10
00:00:58,599 --> 00:01:03,015
At the end, the handler returns. The program restarts at i 2.

11
00:01:03,465 --> 00:01:09,759
This is the basic part: an unplanned function call, and a return to the point of interruption.

12
00:01:10,209 --> 00:01:17,569
Now the tricky part. The program did not call the handler, so it could not prepare. It saved no registers.

13
00:01:18,019 --> 00:01:24,163
So the hardware must save the P C. And the handler must save every register that it changes.

14
00:01:24,613 --> 00:01:29,114
The slides use interrupt as the general word. There are three kinds.

15
00:01:29,564 --> 00:01:42,833
A synchronous interrupt is also called an exception. It is tied to one instruction, for example an overflow. The faulting instruction cannot be finished, so the hardware must handle it at once.

16
00:01:43,283 --> 00:01:56,126
An asynchronous interrupt is also called an interrupt. It is an external event, not tied to any instruction. The hardware has some freedom when to handle it, but it cannot wait forever.

17
00:01:56,576 --> 00:02:06,368
A trap is a system call. The program runs an instruction that causes an exception on purpose. In RISC-V, this instruction is e call.

18
00:02:06,818 --> 00:02:10,039
The slide asks: why not use jal instead?

19
00:02:10,489 --> 00:02:17,913
A model answer. The jal target is P C plus an offset, so the program must know where the handler is.

20
00:02:18,363 --> 00:02:25,467
A trap uses the exception path. The hardware saves the P C and selects the handler address itself.

21
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:34,408
Now put exceptions into the five stage pipeline. The slides call the execute stage E. In this course we write X.

22
00:02:34,858 --> 00:02:41,023
Each kind of exception is found in a different stage. A bad P C address is found in F.

23
00:02:41,473 --> 00:02:43,863
An illegal opcode is found in D.

24
00:02:44,313 --> 00:02:46,361
An overflow is found in X.

25
00:02:46,811 --> 00:02:49,200
A bad data address is found in M.

26
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:54,066
Asynchronous interrupts are different. They can arrive in any cycle.

27
00:02:54,516 --> 00:03:02,815
Now an example with a problem. Load word number 1 has a bad data address. It is found in M, in cycle 4.

28
00:03:03,265 --> 00:03:09,238
Instruction number 2 has an illegal opcode. It is found in D, in cycle 3.

29
00:03:09,688 --> 00:03:16,472
So the younger instruction is found first. But the handler must see number 1 first, in program order.

30
00:03:16,922 --> 00:03:24,431
So the slide asks two questions. One: how to handle several exceptions in different stages at the same time.

31
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:27,804
Two: how and where to handle external interrupts.

32
00:03:28,254 --> 00:03:33,545
The I S A defines a serial order. Instructions run one after another.

33
00:03:33,995 --> 00:03:39,264
So an interrupt falls between two instructions: between i 1 and i 2.

34
00:03:39,714 --> 00:03:48,098
But the pipeline overlaps instructions. At the moment of the interrupt, i 1, i 2 and i 3 are all in progress.

35
00:03:48,548 --> 00:03:50,895
The interrupt line cuts through all three.

36
00:03:51,345 --> 00:03:55,825
A precise interrupt must still seem to happen between two instructions.

37
00:03:56,275 --> 00:04:00,115
So the older instruction, i 1, finishes completely.

38
00:04:00,565 --> 00:04:05,706
The younger instructions, i 2 and i 3, act as if they never happened.

39
00:04:06,156 --> 00:04:14,881
Now a state that is not precise. x 5 is 7. Before the interrupt, i 3 adds 1, and writes 8 into x 5.

40
00:04:15,331 --> 00:04:23,801
The handler returns to i 2, so i 3 runs again. x 5 becomes 9, but it must be 8. The result is wrong.

41
00:04:24,251 --> 00:04:30,011
Question 1 asks how to handle several exceptions in different stages at the same time.

42
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:36,669
The answer is a commit point. The slides put it in M, inside the data memory access.

43
00:04:37,119 --> 00:04:46,377
Choose the commit point before any state element changes. The data memory is one state element. The commit point comes before its write.

44
00:04:46,827 --> 00:04:52,865
The register file is the other state element. It is written in W, after the commit point.

45
00:04:53,315 --> 00:05:00,291
Each stage can raise an exception flag. F, D, X and M each find one kind of exception.

46
00:05:00,741 --> 00:05:06,650
The flag does not act at once. It goes into the pipeline register, with the instruction.

47
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:11,068
At each later stage, a new flag joins the old ones.

48
00:05:11,518 --> 00:05:14,953
So the flags reach the commit point in program order.

49
00:05:15,403 --> 00:05:24,256
Each instruction also carries its own P C down the pipeline. At the commit point, the hardware knows which instruction faulted.

50
00:05:24,706 --> 00:05:42,007
At the commit point, the hardware writes two registers. The first is E P C, the exception P C. It gets the P C of the interrupted instruction. The P C itself must change to the handler, so without E P C, the way back is lost.

51
00:05:42,457 --> 00:05:46,873
The second register is Cause. It gets the reason for the exception.

52
00:05:47,323 --> 00:05:51,035
Asynchronous interrupts are also checked at the commit point.

53
00:05:51,485 --> 00:05:58,141
Then the commit logic kills the instruction in M, and all younger ones in X, D and F.

54
00:05:58,591 --> 00:06:01,471
And it selects the handler P C for the next fetch.

55
00:06:01,921 --> 00:06:09,132
Now animate an exception. Five instructions run with no hazards. Cycles 1 to 3 are normal.

56
00:06:09,582 --> 00:06:18,926
In cycle 4, add number 2 is in X, and it overflows. The hardware only sets the exception flag. Nothing else happens yet.

57
00:06:19,376 --> 00:06:23,216
In cycle 5, number 2 reaches the commit point M.

58
00:06:23,666 --> 00:06:28,509
Number 1 is older. It is in W in cycle 5, and it finishes.

59
00:06:28,959 --> 00:06:35,017
Now the hardware acts. E P C gets the P C of number 2. Cause gets overflow.

60
00:06:35,467 --> 00:06:42,166
In the same cycle, the hardware kills number 2 in M, the load in X, sub in D, and or in F.

61
00:06:42,616 --> 00:06:51,213
From cycle 6, the killed instructions show dashes. Number 2 does not write back. The younger instructions change nothing.

62
00:06:51,663 --> 00:06:55,567
In cycle 6, F fetches the first handler instruction.

63
00:06:56,017 --> 00:07:03,868
The notes on the right sum it up. The older instruction finishes. The faulting instruction and the younger ones are flushed.

64
00:07:04,318 --> 00:07:07,731
After the handler, the program restarts at number 2.

65
00:07:08,181 --> 00:07:16,715
Now, priority. Case a: one instruction raises two flags. The first is a bad P C address, found in F.

66
00:07:17,165 --> 00:07:20,301
The second is an illegal opcode, found in D.

67
00:07:20,751 --> 00:07:29,135
The fetch address was bad, so the bits that D decodes are garbage. The illegal opcode is only a result of the first fault.

68
00:07:29,585 --> 00:07:34,833
So Cause is P C address. Within one instruction, the earliest stage wins.

69
00:07:35,283 --> 00:07:43,667
Case b: two instructions fault in the same cycle. In cycle 4, load word is in M, with a bad data address.

70
00:07:44,117 --> 00:07:48,341
In the same cycle 4, add is in X, and it overflows.

71
00:07:48,791 --> 00:07:56,983
Load word is older, and it is at the commit point now. So load word wins. Add is killed, and its flag never reaches M.

72
00:07:57,433 --> 00:08:00,739
So, within one instruction, the earliest stage wins.

73
00:08:01,189 --> 00:08:06,245
Between instructions, the oldest one wins. It reaches the commit point first.

74
00:08:06,695 --> 00:08:10,663
Question 2 asks how and where to handle external interrupts.

75
00:08:11,113 --> 00:08:17,023
The slide answer: check them at the commit point, M. A request arrives in cycle c.

76
00:08:17,473 --> 00:08:25,217
The hardware treats the instruction in M, i 2, as interrupted. E P C gets the P C of i 2.

77
00:08:25,667 --> 00:08:30,147
The younger instructions i 3, i 4 and i 5 are killed.

78
00:08:30,597 --> 00:08:33,242
The older i 1 in W finishes.

79
00:08:33,692 --> 00:08:38,193
In cycle c plus 1, F fetches the first handler instruction.

80
00:08:38,643 --> 00:08:45,257
After the handler, the program restarts at i 2. The P C gets the value in E P C.

81
00:08:45,707 --> 00:08:51,061
An exception uses the same path: E P C, kill, and the handler P C.

82
00:08:51,511 --> 00:08:59,063
A summary, with the example from before. In cycle 5, the faulting add, number 2, is in M. It is killed.

83
00:08:59,513 --> 00:09:03,844
The three younger instructions, in X, D and F, are killed too.

84
00:09:04,294 --> 00:09:07,963
The older add, number 1, is in W. It finishes.

85
00:09:08,413 --> 00:09:15,965
E P C gets the P C of number 2, and Cause gets overflow. In cycle 6, F fetches the handler.

86
00:09:16,415 --> 00:09:26,143
Number 2 was found in X in cycle 4. The hardware acted on it in M in cycle 5. 5 minus 4 is 1 cycle later.

87
00:09:26,593 --> 00:09:35,297
The hardware always acts at the commit point, M. Each cycle, an instruction moves one stage. So count the stages to M.

88
00:09:35,747 --> 00:09:41,849
A fault found in F moves 3 stages to M. So the hardware acts 3 cycles later.

89
00:09:42,299 --> 00:09:46,395
A fault found in D moves 2 stages: 2 cycles later.

90
00:09:46,845 --> 00:09:52,157
A fault found in M is already at the commit point. The hardware acts at once.

91
00:09:52,607 --> 00:09:57,833
Compare a wrong branch guess from video 11. The branch resolves in X.

92
00:09:58,283 --> 00:10:00,438
Only F and D are flushed.

93
00:10:00,888 --> 00:10:03,661
The branch and the older instructions go on.

94
00:10:04,111 --> 00:10:10,575
No E P C is saved, and no Cause. F fetches the correct target in the next cycle.

95
00:10:11,025 --> 00:10:21,137
Now the spring 2020 quiz, question 3. Interrupts happen at a high frequency, so context saving and restoring must be fast.

96
00:10:21,587 --> 00:10:30,291
The context is the 31 registers x 1 to x 31. x 0 is always 0, so it is not saved.

97
00:10:30,741 --> 00:10:33,749
Normally, the handler saves them in main memory.

98
00:10:34,199 --> 00:10:38,551
This quiz resolves interrupts and exceptions at the W stage.

99
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:44,783
The slides use M. The method is the same: one commit point, and act only there.

100
00:10:45,233 --> 00:10:48,646
There are five parts. Parts A and B have no cache.

101
00:10:49,096 --> 00:10:54,408
Part C adds a data cache. It uses the cache ideas from video 14.

102
00:10:54,858 --> 00:11:03,669
Part A. With no cache, describe one way to save and restore the P C when an interrupt happens. Pause the video and try it.

103
00:11:04,119 --> 00:11:11,756
Follow the slides. First, carry the P C of each instruction down the pipeline, in every pipeline register.

104
00:11:12,206 --> 00:11:17,155
Carry it up to the commit point. In this quiz, the commit point is W.

105
00:11:17,605 --> 00:11:22,597
At W, write E P C with the P C of the interrupted instruction.

106
00:11:23,047 --> 00:11:25,159
Also write Cause with the reason.

107
00:11:25,609 --> 00:11:29,300
Kill the younger instructions in F, D, X and M.

108
00:11:29,750 --> 00:11:32,865
The instruction in W does not write back.

109
00:11:33,315 --> 00:11:36,109
Then the P C select picks the handler address.

110
00:11:36,559 --> 00:11:46,757
To restore, add a return instruction at the end of the handler. It copies E P C into the P C. Fetch restarts at the interrupted instruction.

111
00:11:47,207 --> 00:11:55,569
Part B adds two new instructions. Save context saves all 31 registers. Load context restores them.

112
00:11:56,019 --> 00:12:00,115
The register file gets two sets, set 0 and set 1.

113
00:12:00,565 --> 00:12:04,213
A bit s selects the current set. Now s is 0.

114
00:12:04,663 --> 00:12:10,658
The copy operation copies the current set s into the other set, s X O R 1.

115
00:12:11,108 --> 00:12:17,529
Now set 1 holds the same values as set 0: 5, 9, and so on, to 7.

116
00:12:17,979 --> 00:12:25,745
The swap operation changes s to s X O R 1. Now s is 1, so set 1 is the current set.

117
00:12:26,195 --> 00:12:31,357
If both come in the same cycle, the register file copies first, then swaps.

118
00:12:31,807 --> 00:12:34,986
This design does not support nested interrupts.

119
00:12:35,436 --> 00:12:40,087
We use the copy for save context, and the swap for load context.

120
00:12:40,537 --> 00:12:48,366
Part B 1. How does an application benefit from save context and load context? Pause the video and try it.

121
00:12:48,816 --> 00:12:55,280
Without them, the handler saves the context with 31 store words, one for each register.

122
00:12:55,730 --> 00:12:59,890
At the end, it restores the context with 31 load words.

123
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:05,801
31 plus 31 is 62 memory instructions for each interrupt.

124
00:13:06,251 --> 00:13:11,606
With them, the handler uses 2 instructions: save context and load context.

125
00:13:12,056 --> 00:13:17,453
The copy and the swap happen inside the register file, with no memory access.

126
00:13:17,903 --> 00:13:24,794
So each interrupt costs much less time. This matters, because interrupts come at a high frequency.

127
00:13:25,244 --> 00:13:34,695
Part B 2. Which changes does the pipeline need, apart from the register file? Go stage by stage. Pause the video and try it.

128
00:13:35,145 --> 00:13:39,881
F needs no change. The new instructions are fetched like all others.

129
00:13:40,331 --> 00:13:54,368
D decodes the two new opcodes. It makes two new control bits: copy for save context, and swap for load context. The instructions write no register, so Reg W En is 0.

130
00:13:54,818 --> 00:14:02,541
X and M do no work. They only carry the two bits down the pipeline registers, like Reg W En.

131
00:14:02,991 --> 00:14:07,833
W is the commit point, so the copy or swap happens in W.

132
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:20,678
All older instructions have written their results, so the copy is complete. And if an interrupt kills save context, it has changed nothing. Younger instructions have not written yet.

133
00:14:21,128 --> 00:14:31,411
One hazard remains. After load context swaps the sets, the younger instructions in D, X and M have read registers from the old set.

134
00:14:31,861 --> 00:14:38,901
Flush F, D, X and M, and fetch again after load context. This is like a wrong branch guess.

135
00:14:39,351 --> 00:14:48,545
Part B 3. The change breaks some existing interrupt handlers. Describe the problem, and how to fix it. Pause the video and try it.

136
00:14:48,995 --> 00:14:55,737
Trace the two sets. P means the program registers. At the start, the program uses set 0.

137
00:14:56,187 --> 00:15:02,181
Interrupt 1 runs save context. It is a copy, so set 1 also holds P.

138
00:15:02,631 --> 00:15:07,218
Handler 1 changes set 0. Set 0 now holds H 1.

139
00:15:07,668 --> 00:15:22,452
Now an existing handler enables interrupts again, and a nested interrupt comes. Handler 2 runs save context. The copy writes H 1 into set 1. The saved program registers P are lost.

140
00:15:22,902 --> 00:15:25,761
Handler 2 changes set 0 to H 2.

141
00:15:26,211 --> 00:15:34,659
Handler 2 runs load context. The swap makes s equal to 1. Handler 1 gets H 1 back, which is correct.

142
00:15:35,109 --> 00:15:44,965
Then handler 1 runs load context. It swaps back to set 0. The program resumes with the registers of handler 2. That is wrong.

143
00:15:45,415 --> 00:15:57,041
The fix: keep interrupts disabled from save context to load context. A handler that must allow nested interrupts uses store word and load word, as before.

144
00:15:57,491 --> 00:16:11,145
A second problem: load context restores all 31 registers. So a handler can no longer return a result in a register, for example after e call. It must return it through memory.

145
00:16:11,595 --> 00:16:22,603
Part C adds a data cache. Acquire context reserves an address range for the context. The lines of the range stay in the cache until release context.

146
00:16:23,053 --> 00:16:31,821
Part C uses the cache ideas from video 14: blocks, sets, tags, and L R U. Pause the video and try it.

147
00:16:32,271 --> 00:16:40,932
First, the size. Each register has 4 bytes. 31 registers times 4 bytes is 124 bytes.

148
00:16:41,382 --> 00:16:47,462
The quiz gives no block size. Assume 32 byte blocks, as in the lecture example.

149
00:16:47,912 --> 00:16:56,360
Take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 0 0, a multiple of 32. Then the range fills 4 lines.

150
00:16:56,810 --> 00:17:04,106
Each line holds 32 over 4, so 8 registers. The first line holds x 1 to x 8.

151
00:17:04,556 --> 00:17:10,828
The last line holds x 25 to x 31. Its last 4 bytes are unused.

152
00:17:11,278 --> 00:17:18,574
Now take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 1 0. The range starts 16 bytes into a line.

153
00:17:19,024 --> 00:17:21,499
Now the range touches 5 lines.

154
00:17:21,949 --> 00:17:31,954
So a start more than 4 bytes into a line gives 5 lines. The hardware must handle every line from the first to the last address of the range.

155
00:17:32,404 --> 00:17:36,628
Now the pipeline, stage by stage. F needs no change.

156
00:17:37,078 --> 00:17:47,211
D decodes the two new opcodes and reads r s 1. It makes two control bits, acquire and release. The instructions write no register.

157
00:17:47,661 --> 00:17:55,171
X computes the start address. The A L U passes r s 1, as for a load word with offset 0.

158
00:17:55,621 --> 00:18:06,095
M is the main change: the instructions use the data cache in M. Acquire fills each line of the range and locks it. Release unlocks the lines.

159
00:18:06,545 --> 00:18:11,303
A fill is a cache miss, so the pipeline stalls, as for a load word miss.

160
00:18:11,753 --> 00:18:15,721
W does nothing. The instructions write no register.

161
00:18:16,171 --> 00:18:23,147
To save the context, the handler still uses 31 store words, and later 31 load words.

162
00:18:23,597 --> 00:18:29,719
They access the data cache in M, and they always hit. The locked lines never leave the cache.

163
00:18:30,169 --> 00:18:34,287
Acquire context runs only once, when the program starts.

164
00:18:34,737 --> 00:18:40,347
Now the cache. Take a 2 way set associative cache: way 0 and way 1.

165
00:18:40,797 --> 00:18:44,275
The cache has 4 sets, set 0 to set 3.

166
00:18:44,725 --> 00:18:51,381
Each frame has a valid bit V, a tag, and a 32 byte block, as in video 14.

167
00:18:51,831 --> 00:18:56,759
Add one new bit to every frame: a lock bit, L. It starts at 0.

168
00:18:57,209 --> 00:19:05,699
Acquire context goes through each line of the range. On a miss, it fills the line from memory. Then it sets L to 1.

169
00:19:06,149 --> 00:19:15,920
The set comes from the address bits. A 32 byte block gives 5 offset bits, bits 4 to 0, because 32 is 2 to the 5.

170
00:19:16,370 --> 00:19:26,951
The 4 sets give 2 index bits, bits 6 and 5, because 4 is 2 to the 2. This is the same as the line address over 32, mod 4.

171
00:19:27,401 --> 00:19:42,271
The lines start at hex 1 0 0 0, 32 bytes apart. So their index bits count 0 0, 0 1, 1 0, 1 1. The 4 lines fall into sets 0, 1, 2 and 3.

172
00:19:42,721 --> 00:19:49,377
So acquire context fills way 0 of sets 0 to 3, and sets L to 1 in each frame.

173
00:19:49,827 --> 00:20:00,152
The replacement policy, for example L R U, must never pick a frame with L equal to 1. So a locked line stays in the cache until release.

174
00:20:00,602 --> 00:20:07,365
One more check. Each set needs at least one unlocked way. Way 1 stays free for the other blocks.

175
00:20:07,815 --> 00:20:16,732
In a direct mapped cache, each set has only one way. The other blocks of a locked set cannot be cached, so they must go to memory.

176
00:20:17,182 --> 00:20:29,086
Also, take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 1 0. The range touches 5 lines, from hex 1 0 0 0 to hex 1 0 8 0.

177
00:20:29,536 --> 00:20:39,307
Lines hex 1 0 0 0 and hex 1 0 8 0 both have index bits 0 0. So both map to set 0.

178
00:20:39,757 --> 00:20:44,429
So both ways of set 0 lock, and set 0 has no free way.

179
00:20:44,879 --> 00:20:48,356
So r s 1 must be a multiple of 32.

180
00:20:48,806 --> 00:20:54,353
Release context sets L back to 0. The line becomes a normal block again.

181
00:20:54,803 --> 00:21:00,797
In a write back cache, the line is written to memory when it is replaced, if it is dirty.

182
00:21:01,247 --> 00:21:08,031
Last, compare the cost for each interrupt of the three designs: no cache, part C, and part B.

183
00:21:08,481 --> 00:21:16,225
With no cache, the handler runs 31 store words and 31 load words. Each one waits for main memory.

184
00:21:16,675 --> 00:21:24,014
With part C, the same 31 plus 31, so 62 instructions, always hit in the data cache.

185
00:21:24,464 --> 00:21:31,803
Assume a hit takes 1 cycle in M. 62 hits times 1 cycle is about 62 cycles.

186
00:21:32,253 --> 00:21:41,106
With part B, save context and load context are only 2 instructions, plus one flush. So part B is the fastest.

187
00:21:41,556 --> 00:21:50,623
Part C keeps the register file unchanged and has no swap hazard. But it still runs 62 instructions for each interrupt.

188
00:21:51,073 --> 00:21:57,643
Part B needs a second register set and a flush after load context, and it allows no nesting.

189
00:21:58,093 --> 00:22:06,349
Recap. One. An interrupt is an unplanned call to a handler. The hardware saves the restart P C in E P C.

190
00:22:06,799 --> 00:22:15,589
Two. An exception is tied to one instruction, which cannot finish. An interrupt is external. A trap is an exception on purpose.

191
00:22:16,039 --> 00:22:23,377
Three. The pipeline finds exceptions in F, D, X and M. So it finds them out of program order.

192
